// ===================== 8.3 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('8.3  What is wrong with it, and I still recommend it'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open, and the instruction is the exercise: **say the worst thing, in its strongest form, and then do not add _but_.** ⚠ **Time the three seconds yourself**, out loud if necessary — _one, two, three_ — because almost nobody manages them and the room can hear the difference the moment somebody does. A class that can hold that silence has learnt something that will matter for the whole of Unit 9.'));
out.push(A('**b** Real weaknesses: **2** and **4** — both could change somebody\'s mind. Decoration: **1**, **3** and **5**. ⚠ _It is perhaps a little expensive_ fails for one reason only: **there is no figure in it.** With a figure it is a weakness; without one it is a courtesy, and the class should be able to say which it is in four seconds by the end of this stage.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — whether the weakness **could change somebody\'s mind**; and a review with no weakness in it is not believed and should not be, **because the reader knows that everything has one** and concludes that the writer has not used the thing.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** That an answer in the same breath **takes the weakness away and the reader notices** — and everything else in the review is then read as a defence.   **2** That with a one-handed cap she caps it without looking and loses it about once a week; **the two-handed cap makes her look at it**, and in seven weeks she has not lost one.'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **Two things in this cycle and the first is not about writing at all.** Saying a weakness in its strongest form, with no _but_, and then allowing three seconds of silence, is a thing most adults cannot do on a first attempt — and the inability is not shyness: **it is the fear of having the weakness believed.** Name that, once, and then drill the silence. ⚠ The second thing is the shape of the separation: **_its weakness is real and I would still …, because …_ does not answer the weakness.** The cap is still awkward in a lift; what the sentence claims is that **the weakness does not touch the reason** — a different claim and a much stronger one. Mark Stage 3 on exactly that, and refuse every separation of the form _but you get used to it_, which is the commonest and takes the weakness back.'));
out.push(NOTE('**The test for a real weakness is one question: could it change somebody\'s mind?** _Expensive_ could, with a figure on it; _a little expensive_ could not. ⚠ Mrs Khoury\'s figure is the one to quote: she takes out about five decorative weaknesses a month and **has never once had a writer object**, which tells you they knew. **And the danger worth being in is the other one:** if the strongest form of the weakness would stop your reader buying the thing, **then it should** — and the writer has learnt something about their own recommendation that no amount of rewriting would have told them. Expect one student in a class of twelve to discover exactly that, and treat it as the best outcome of the cycle rather than as a failed review.'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**1** _(a tool)_ _The case will not go in a counter drawer, so it lives on a shelf behind me — three seconds a repair, about four hundred repairs a year._ **Its weakness is real and I would still buy it, because the bits do not round off, and the two things are not connected.**',
  '**2** _(a method)_ _Writing the five lines costs four minutes of an evening I have already finished._ **Its weakness is real and I would still do it, because the one dispute it has prevented would have taken a fortnight.**',
  '**3** _(a book)_ _The binding failed in a month and mine is in a rubber band._ **Its weakness is real and I would still buy it — and not because it was cheap.**',
  '**4** _(a shop)_ _Single items cost about a third more than the per-item price in a box._ **Its weakness is real and I would still go there, because eleven spares in a cupboard are money I have not got.**',
  '**5** _(something you were given)_ _It weighs four kilos and I carry it between two buildings twice a day._ ⚠ **Its weakness is real and I do not use it**, which is the honest version and the one the class will avoid.',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ Mark the **separation** and nothing else. Items **1** and **2** have the ideal shape: a measured weakness, then a reason that **does not touch it**. Item **3** is the one to read out, because the refusal — _and not because it was cheap_ — is the move that costs one clause and buys the paragraph. ⚠ And item **5** is the case the class will not write: a weakness that **does** change the verdict. **Say that it is the right answer when the weakness really is that strong**, or students will learn to pick weaknesses they can survive.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
