out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The three last lines for each of the eight, and which the partner chose. The count: **the smallest trial is chosen in only one or two of the eight** and is the one nobody arrives with. ⚠ And about a third of the thresholds offered have **no number in them** — _if you are busy_, _if you use it a lot_ — which is _it depends_ with a full stop. Send those back for a figure and the second attempt always has one.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**a** _**Test:** fill in one of the two-page forms on a live call while somebody times you. **Threshold:** over a quarter of your calls from repeat callers, adopt it; under a tenth, do not. **Smallest trial:** run it on one of the three teams for a month and count the escalations. **Chosen:** the threshold, because it is the only one of the three that can be done without anybody\'s permission._',
  '**b** _On Monday, because of my partner\'s line, I would **count how many handsets I opened last week** — which is a figure I could get from the fault book in four minutes. ⚠ **And I would not have counted anything because of his first version**, which was that the set is well worth the money._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Jad has figures for this that nobody else in the room has. He went back through forty reviews on the forum, his own and other people\'s, and sorted them by their last line. The ones ending in a test or a figure got replies saying "I tried it" or "mine came out at eleven"; the ones ending in a recommendation got agreement, which is not the same thing. Twenty-six of the forty ended in a recommendation, and of those twenty-six, four produced a reply showing that anybody had done anything. Of the other fourteen, eleven did. Four of twenty-six against eleven of fourteen, which is as close to proof as this class is going to get about anything. So the last line is not a summary and not a repetition of the verdict: it is the thing the reader does next, and if there is nothing for them to do then the review has ended on a courtesy — which is 5.3 in a new genre. Dima named the three kinds that work: the test, borrow one for a day and try capping it in a lift; the threshold, under five a week buy the cheap set; and the smallest trial, buy one and not six. And the third is the most under-used, because a reader told to buy one instead of six has been given permission to find out cheaply, and nobody selling anything has ever said it. Omar asked what makes a test a good test, and the answer is that the reader can do it this week, by themselves, without you: a test that needs the reviewer\'s help, or a month, or somebody else\'s permission is not a last line but an invitation to correspond. And Hiba asked about thresholds, and the number has to be in it, because a lot is not a number: a threshold with no figure on it is the same sentence as it depends, and it is the commonest way a good review ends badly. Mazen asked whether the last line can be the weakness, and it can, and it is a strong ending when the weakness is the test — if you cannot keep the case on the counter, do not buy it. What the last line must never be is the verdict again, because said twice a verdict is a verdict the writer did not trust. And the three kinds to have by heart are the test, the threshold with a number, and the smallest trial — of which the smallest trial is the most under-used and the most trusted, because a reader told to buy one instead of six has been given permission to find out cheaply, and nobody selling anything has ever said it.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the partner\'s chosen last line and what the student would do on Monday. ⚠ Collect the _nothings_ and count them. The figure falls sharply between Stage 1 and Stage 4 — typically from nine or ten in twelve to two or three — **and that fall is the cycle, measured on the class in one lesson.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** _Overall, I would recommend it._ → **✓** a test, a threshold with a number, or a smallest trial.   **✗** _If you use it a lot, buy it._ → **✓** a figure. _A lot_ is not a number.   **✗** A test that needs the reviewer. → **✓** this week, alone, without you.   **✗** The verdict repeated as the last line. → **✓** a different job. Said twice, a verdict is one the writer did not trust.   **✗** A sentence of thanks after the last line. → **✓** nothing after it; whatever is last **is** the last line.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read'));
out.push(A('**1** By their **last line only**, and he counted **replies that showed somebody had done something** — _I tried it_, _mine came out at eleven_, _I borrowed one_.'));
out.push(A('**2** He is **not** claiming the last line caused it: the reviews ending in a test are probably better throughout and he cannot separate the two things with forty posts. ⚠ **What he can say is that the two groups are not close**, and that nothing else he sorted by — length, author, subject — produced a difference at all.'));
out.push(A('**3** That the three **smallest-trial** endings produced seven replies between them, the highest rate of any group — and that in nineteen reviews he has **never once used it.** His own worst line is printed: _all in all, a sound piece of equipment that I think most people would be happy with_ — sixteen words, no replies, **and _most people_ is the emptiest phrase in it.**'));
