// ===================== 8.7 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('8.7  Review & Mediate: Two reviews that disagree'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready – Unit 8 quiz'));
out.push(A('**1** _**For** anybody who reads at a gate, this is the one._   **2** _Where the big one is complete, the thin one **is** findable._   **3** _The difference is not better and worse **but** how often you look things up._   **4** _What decided it **was** the hold button._   **5** _The cheap set did not **live** up to the price._   **6** _Borrow one and see how it **stacks** up against yours._'));
out.push(A('⚠ Items **1** and **3** are the two that carry the unit. **1** is a preposition and looks trivial: _to anybody_ addresses a person you are speaking to, and _for anybody_ names a **class of reader**, which is the whole of 8.1. **3** is the _not … but_ frame and students write _than_ because they can hear a comparative in the sentence. Item **6** is from the declared set — _stack up against_ — and _stand up against_ is real English meaning something else, which is why the option was removed from the printed item.'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** Open — the two reviews are read as they were sent, with the note underneath them and the line neither of them contains.'));
out.push(A('**b** The disagreement **disappears**: read one after the other, the two repaired verdicts agree, because they are two sentences about two different jobs. And two reviews really contradict each other **only when both reviewers used the thing for the same task, under the same conditions, and still disagree.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **This is the fourth mediation of the course and the fourth kind, and the class should be told so: in 4.7 both parties had overclaimed; in 6.7 the record was silent; in 7.7 the document was defective; here both reviews are true and both are incomplete, and what is missing is the same thing in each — six words at the front.** The third move is new and it is the one to drill: **supply the missing first line of each review and read them again.** ⚠ Then protect the second half of the cycle, which is the one students will skip: **the test for a real contradiction.** Two reviews of the same task in the same conditions that still disagree are not to be repaired — they are evidence that **something neither reviewer has mentioned is varying**, and that is Unit 7 arriving inside Unit 8. **Pair B in Stage 3 is exactly that case and about half the class will try to repair it.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(A('No Practise line in x.7: the Language box here **tests** rather than drills, and the testing is done in Stage 3 on two pairs of reviews the class has not seen — **one repairable and one not.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Pair A)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '**What review one says.** _"Indispensable — I use it every day."_',
  '**What review two says.** _"Thin and disappointing; a third of the words I wanted were not in it."_',
  '**What neither review contains.** A reader. Neither says what the writer was doing with the dictionary.',
  '**What each was doing.** One checks spellings at a counter — common words, quickly, many times a day. One was reading a technical article and wanted terms.',
  '**The two reviews, with their first lines supplied.** **"For anybody checking the spelling of a common word at a counter, this is indispensable."** **"For anybody reading technical prose, a third of the words you want will not be in it."**',
  '**And read one after the other, there is no disagreement at all.** ⚠ The settling figure already exists: **the publisher\'s word count, printed on the back** — about eighteen thousand entries, which is a counter dictionary and not a technical one, and neither reviewer looked at it.',
  '**The test, applied.** Different jobs, different conditions. **Not a real contradiction**, and the repair is two first lines.',
]));
out.push(A('⚠ **Pair B is the real contradiction and must not be repaired.** Both reviewers open the same handsets, about twenty a week, at a counter — **the same task in the same conditions** — and one reports four hundred handsets with the bits still biting while the other had two bits round off in a fortnight. **There is no reader to supply.** What is varying is something neither has mentioned, and the likely candidates are a batch, a counterfeit, or the shop: **neither review says where the set was bought**, and that is the sentence the mediator asks for. ⚠ About half the class will supply first lines for Pair B anyway; **name that as the fault of the stage and not as a small slip**, because repairing a real contradiction hides a mechanism.'));
out.push(S('Stage 3R · Perform – listener task'));
out.push(A('The three moves for each pair, and which pair is the real contradiction. ⚠ **Pair B**, and what neither reviewer mentions is **which shop they bought the set from.** The count: about half the pairs repair Pair B instead of recognising it. Ask those pairs one question — _what were they each doing with it?_ — and they answer _the same thing_, which is the test giving its own answer.'));
out.push(S('Stage 4 · Your turn (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE([
  '_**My thing:** the two-page form. **Somebody whose verdict differs:** Omar, who has used it at the print counter and says it is a page of work for nothing._',
  '_**Both verdicts.** Mine: it is the form to use. His: it is forty seconds a call spent on nothing._',
  '_**Both first lines supplied.** "For anybody who will be asked six months later what was promised, this is the form." "For anybody whose customer is standing in front of them and the matter closes while they are there, it is forty seconds a call for nothing."_',
  '_**Read one after the other: no disagreement.** ⚠ **And the test says so properly**, because we are not doing the same job in the same conditions: my calls come back and his do not. **If his calls came back and he still said it was work for nothing, I should be looking for what neither of us had mentioned** — and the first candidate would be that he keeps the promise somewhere else._',
]));
out.push(S('Stage 4R · Report – mediation (sample)'));
out.push(SAMPLE(['Two reviews of one instrument, six months apart, contradicting each other flatly. Asked which of them is wrong, Omar said neither — they are different readers, which the class had done in 8.1. So he did the mediation, which is three moves and not the moves of 7.7: quote both verdicts, name what neither review contains, and then supply the missing first line of each and read them again. For anybody who takes a reading at a gate while a driver is signing, this is an excellent instrument. For anybody walking a cold store with eleven readings to take, it is useless. And read one after the other, there is no disagreement at all: they agree, and they are two sentences about two different jobs. Which is the finding of the cycle and is worth more than it looks — most disagreements between two honest reviews are not disagreements, but two verdicts with the readers left out, and the readers were the only things that differed. Dima said she sees about two a month and used to write to both reviewers; now she writes to one and asks what they were doing with it, and nobody has ever been offended by that question or failed to explain the contradiction. Hiba said there must be real disagreements, and there are, and the test is exact: two reviews really contradict each other only when both reviewers used the thing for the same task, under the same conditions, and still disagree. That is rarer than anybody expects, and when it happens the two reviews together are worth more than either alone, because the disagreement has to rest on something neither of them has thought to mention — a thing that varies between units of the same product, or a skill nobody named. A real contradiction is a mechanism nobody has looked for yet — a thing that varies between units of the same product, or a skill nobody named. And Mazen asked whether the mediator decides which review is right, and there is nothing to decide, which is the third time this book has said so in three different ways: in 4.7 both parties had overclaimed, in 6.7 the record was silent, in 7.7 the document was defective, and here both reviews are true and both are incomplete, and what is missing is the same thing in each — six words at the front, which is 5.1. Lubna said so, and Ms Huda said it is the last time this book will make the point: the first six words decide what a sentence is about, and in a review they decide whose sentence it is.']));
out.push(A('Listener task: the two repaired first lines from the student\'s own pair, and whether the contradiction survived them. ⚠ Count the pairs where it **did** survive. There are usually one or two in a class, and those are worth four minutes in front of everybody: ask both students what they were doing with the thing, in the same words, and if the answers match, **the class has found a real contradiction and should be told that it is the more interesting result.**'));
out.push(S('Board clinic – typical mistakes'));
out.push(A('**✗** Deciding which review is right. → **✓** there is nothing to decide; both are true and both are incomplete.   **✗** Repairing a real contradiction. → **✓** apply the test first: same task, same conditions?   **✗** Supplying a first line the evidence does not support. → **✓** mark the inference — _on the face of it, the second depot is reading air in a store_.   **✗** Averaging two reviews. → **✓** ask what each reviewer was doing, and take the one doing your job.'));
out.push(S('Stage 5 · Read – exam practice (B2 First Reading and Use of English Part 3)'));
out.push(A('**1** comparison   **2** weakness   **3** strength   **4** durability   **5** disappointment   **6** satisfaction   **7** improvement   **8** disagreement'));
out.push(A('Two things to say about this task, because it is the first Part 3 of the course. ⚠ **Part 3 is not vocabulary; it is morphology with a grammar test inside it.** The gap tells you the **part of speech** before you know the word: _a_ before gap 1 and gap 2 means a countable noun, _no_ before gap 3 means a noun, and _their_ before gap 8 means a noun again. **Decide the part of speech from the words either side, then build it.** ⚠ And item **8** is the one that separates a class: **AGREE** must become **disagreement** — a prefix **and** a suffix — because the sentence says the thing **disappears** and what disappears is the apparent contradiction. **About half of any class writes _agreement_ and then cannot explain what disappeared.** Teach the habit of reading the whole sentence for sense after building the word, which costs four seconds an item and is the only check this part allows.'));
out.push(S('Stage 6 · Write with help (sample)'));
